    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Focal epilepsy with structural cause (G40.209)</content>. No seizure on levetiracetam. The irritability and tiredness are known effects of levetiracetam and are settling; PHQ-9 6 without low mood or thoughts of self-harm. Continue the same dose. The SUDEP discussion deferred at the last visit was held today. [S]<list><item>Continue levetiracetam 500 mg twice daily.</item><item>Call if irritability worsens or low mood appears: switching to lamotrigine is an option.</item><item>Continue the seizure diary, detection watch and safety plan.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Solitary cerebral cavernous malformation, left orbitofrontal (Q28.3)</content>. She chose lesionectomy after hearing the chance of freedom from seizures, the small risks of the operation, and the option of medication alone. [S]<list><item>Craniotomy in three weeks; continue levetiracetam through and after surgery.</item><item>No training for two weeks after surgery.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and behavior on levetiracetam (PHQ-9)</td><td>Before starting and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 6, from 3: irritability and tiredness on levetiracetam, easing; no thoughts of self-harm</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure diary and adherence</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No seizure; every dose taken</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurosurgical evaluation of an epileptogenic cavernous malformation</td><td>When a cavernous malformation causes epilepsy</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Seen last week; lesionectomy chosen and booked</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>SUDEP counseling</td><td>At diagnosis of epilepsy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Discussed today, three weeks after the diagnosis (deviation recorded at the last visit): what SUDEP is, that tonic-clonic seizures are the main risk factor, and that the best protection is no tonic-clonic seizures, with every dose taken, regular sleep and little alcohol; nocturnal supervision or detection. No harm from the delay: no seizure in the interval. Corrective action: SUDEP added to the clinic&apos;s checklist for every new diagnosis of epilepsy</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nocturnal seizure detection</td><td>When seizures may occur in sleep</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Wrist-worn detection watch worn every night, alerting a contact she chose</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving restriction and the California physician report</td><td>From the first seizure until cleared</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Not driving</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure safety: water, heights, sleep and first aid</td><td>At diagnosis and each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; she follows the plan</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Folic acid and contraception</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Folic acid taken daily; no pregnancy planned</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>EEG and MRI with susceptibility-weighted imaging within one week, and an epilepsy consultation within two weeks. Outcome by 2026-05-05: EEG in two days, MRI in six, consultation at two weeks [S]</item><item>No further seizures on an antiseizure medication she tolerates. In progress; target 2026-10-20 [S]</item><item>Neurosurgical evaluation of the cavernous malformation and a shared decision about lesionectomy within two months. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>Safety plan in place: no driving until cleared, water and height safety, SUDEP discussion, and a nocturnal seizure detection plan. Outcome by 2026-05-27: Driving stopped and reported as California requires; SUDEP discussed (one visit late, recorded as a deviation); detection watch in use [S]</item><item>Graded return to full sprint training within four months of surgery. In progress; target 2026-10-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Krumholz A, et al. Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (PMID 25901057) (2015); Fisher RS, et al. ILAE official report: a practical clinical definition of epilepsy. Epilepsia 2014;55(4):475-482 (PMID 24730690) (2014); Akers A, et al. Synopsis of Guidelines for the Clinical Management of Cerebral Cavernous Malformations: Consensus Recommendations Based on Systematic Literature Review by the Angioma Alliance Scientific Advisory Board Clinical Experts Panel. Neurosurgery 2017;80(5):665-680 (PMID 28387823) (2017); Harden C, et al. Practice guideline summary: Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy incidence rates and risk factors. Neurology 2017;88(17):1674-1680 (PMID 28438841) (2017); Pack AM, et al. Teratogenesis, Perinatal, and Neurodevelopmental Outcomes After In Utero Exposure to Antiseizure Medication: Practice Guideline From the AAN, AES, and SMFM. Neurology 2024;102(11):e209279 (PMID 38748979) (2024); Beniczky S, et al. Automated seizure detection using wearable devices: A clinical practice guideline of the International League Against Epilepsy and the International Federation of Clinical Neurophysiology. Epilepsia 2021;62(3):632-646 (PMID 33666944) (2021); Governor&apos;s message returning Senate Bill 357 without signature (physician reporting of disorders characterized by lapses of consciousness remains mandatory under Health and Safety Code section 103900) (2024); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Lesionectomy in three weeks. Primary care review about five weeks after surgery. Call at once for another seizure, worsening mood, or any thoughts of self-harm. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-21: five weeks after lesionectomy: recovery, seizure control, return to training. [S]</paragraph></text>

